; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; SIMK CONTAINER - holds ONE artefact and absorbs ALL of its radiation.
;
; Two things make it different from the other three containers:
;
; 1. IT CANNOT BE WORN ON THE BELT. Two separate places say so:
;      * the container itself:  belt = false (cannot be put on the belt at all);
;      * the combo:             belt = false as well (unlike the other combos,
;                               which are belt = true).
;    The engine gate is CInventory::CanPutInBelt (Inventory.cpp:1295-1303):
;        if (!pIItem || !pIItem->Belt()) return false;
;    and the flag comes straight from the section
;    (inventory_item.cpp:170: r_bool, section, "belt", FALSE).
;    So the combo physically cannot be equipped - no script needed.
;
; 2. IT ABSORBS EVERYTHING. Our backpack radiation mechanic IGNORES negative
;    radiation_restore_speed values, so "absorb everything" is expressed as
;    radiation_restore_speed = 0 on the combo: whatever the artefact emits, the
;    combo contributes exactly zero to the backpack dose.
;
; radiation_restore_speed of the EMPTY container is also 0 (not a negative
; "absorption" number like the other containers). That is deliberate: a negative
; value would make an empty SIMK in the backpack REDUCE the actor's radiation
; (the mechanic counts negative contributions only if we allowed them, and the
; value would also show up in the tooltip as healing). The container is neutral
; by itself and only cancels the radiation of the artefact inside it.
;
; Weight 3 kg and cost 13000: it is a portable container, not a premium one.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
[bq_simk_container]:bq_container_base



inv_grid_x                  = 3
inv_grid_y                  = 0
inv_grid_width              = 1
inv_grid_height             = 2
bq_icon_full_x              = 4
